      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14163584/assets/d0d8e956fad2f4ef75498692d353f5508247c79bdd908279119c1789ca358b8d.png)\n\nOrder the sides of the triangle $△ABC$ above from the shortest to the longest.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's find the missing angle $A.$ Since the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A+{90}^{∘}+{46}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={180}^{∘}-{136}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={44}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $∠B$ is the largest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{AC}$ must be the longest side of the triangle. \n\nSince $∠A$ is the smallest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ is the shortest side of the triangle.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nBC<AB<AC\n$$\n\nand therefore, the required order is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{BC},\\overset{―}{AB},\\overset{―}{AC}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
